==Old English==

===Etymology===
From Germanic ''*huzda-'', from Indo-European ''*kuzdho-'' ‘something hidden, hiding-place’, from ''*keu-, *kewə-'' ‘cover, encase’. Cognate with Old Saxon ''hord'' ‘treasure, hiding-place’, Old High German [[hort]] (German [[Hort]] ‘hoard, protection, refuge’), Old Norse [[hodd]] (Swedish [[hudda]] ‘shelter’), Gothic {{term|𐌷𐌿𐌶𐌳|tr=huzd||hidden hoard}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ho:rd/|lang=ang}}

===Noun===
'''hord''' {{n}} (''plural:'' '''hord''')

# a [[hoard]], especially of valuable items, hidden for preservation

